{
  "dom_id": "step-q105351",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>2x+1\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x+1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(0,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0,1)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(-2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14255659/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=2x+1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14255659/assets/92ae4b895d5773f704c1d341c4c6a5b63ce76b1d4f855d9c4830cf1d699c3a98.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,1)$ and $Q(-2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14255659/assets/d953addecdef7bca1294d608a3134548727ebc0856ad13a227de1b2cf0e4dfa6.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ satisfy $y=2x+1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y>2x+1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only.'",
